From 80c8fac1b08c4d121c5eb27175d5086a21c86e8d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yaegerbomb42 <yaeger.james42@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 13:53:44 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] feat(agents): deployment profiles, a second harness adapter,
 community templates

Phase 7 of the Agent Studio brief, stacked on feat/agent-studio.

- lib/agents/profiles.ts   named per-account profiles: harness, model
                           overrides, MCP endpoint bindings. resolveProfile()
                           applies them and only rewrites the endpoint of an
                           MCP server the manifest already declares; a binding
                           for an undeclared server is a warning, never an
                           invented server. Profiles never carry credentials.
- adapters/local-claude.ts DELIBERATELY a stub: real PATH-based capability
                           detection, grants zero tools (the CLI has no per-run
                           allowlist in this build) and refuses deploy with
                           'not implemented'. No fake second harness.
- adapters/registry.ts     one place that lists adapters; routes and UI read it.
- lib/agents/templates.ts  read-only gallery from templates/agents/*.json,
                           validated on load; a broken template is reported in
                           'issues' instead of being offered.
- templates/agents/*.json  triage-bot, pr-reviewer, release-notes.
- routes                   GET/POST /api/agents/profiles, DELETE
                           /api/agents/profiles/{id}, GET /api/agents/templates,
                           POST /api/agents/templates/{id}/import; deploy now
                           accepts { profile } and reports what the profile
                           changed.
- UI                       the community section is a real gallery with Use;
                           the builder has a Profile picker used by Deploy.

Live check found two defects, both fixed here: the shipped templates listed
'Glob', which this harness cannot grant (they would have failed at deploy),
and a refused deploy reported the *swarm* compatibility report for a different
adapter. A regression test now asserts every shipped template is deployable.
